Cell[TextData[{
 "From here on this is an ordinary alphabet, and the whole IBL layer applies \
to it. The quotient itself rides along as the ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Algebra\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "40ab56b9-2ef6-4645-abc2-032b1813f1a6"],
 " key of the pairing object, which is where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["CyclicHochschildDifferential"]], 
    "paclet:ChernSimons/ref/CyclicHochschildDifferential", 
    "ChernSimons Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "b3324462-7c69-4c8b-996a-2f400186b061"],
 " reads the chain-level differential from; for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["CP",
     FontSlant->"Plain"], "2"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "9093dda7-6612-4c82-8e3d-a0851f3cd1a8"],
 " that differential is zero, the model being formal, and the next tutorial \
shows the alphabet where it is not. There are three cyclic words of length \
two, for instance, because ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"1", "\[CenterDot]", "1"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
